[background arg="res://arts/splash art/backgrounds/corredor.png" fade="0.0"]
This is my first day at the SS Columbus. I was assigned the post of Chief Medical Scientist at the ship, for assistance in an escort mission.
I came here in a bit of a rush.
As I have been told, the last Chief Medical Scientist, Dr. Natalia Pulaski,  has died suddenly, in the course of her duties.
I have many thoughts about this, but these must wait.
This must be the bridge of the ship.
The golden, imposing metal doors won’t let me mistake this place for any other than the center of command of the SS Columbus.
\doctor: “Doctor Daphnis, reporting to the bridge.”
audio "res://arts/sfx/vadim_makes_sound-sci-fi-door-unlock-and-hydraulic-open-2-546619.mp3" [volume="-15.0"]
[background arg="res://arts/splash art/backgrounds/sala_de_comando.png" fade="0.5"]
I see lots of officers walking around the bridge, busy with their chores.
join commander (neutro) center
I look for the Captain of the ship, but the only superior present is the Commander, judging from the patent in his uniform.
I look for the Captain of the ship, but the only superior present is the Commander, judging from the patent in his uniform.
The Commander is standing at the center of the control station, looking very official, waiting for me to approach him.
I feel a bit nervous as I walk the full distance to reach him, under his fixed gaze.
He is already measuring me.
\doctor: “Reporting to service, sir.”
commander: “Chief Medical Scientist Daphnis, welcome aboard. I am Commander Flagg, at present in charge of the Columbus.
commander: I trust that your voyage here was safe?”
\doctor: “Yes sir, thank you. I came as soon as possible. Thank you for arranging my travel in that cargo ship.”
commander: “We apologize for not providing comfortable accommodations, but given the emergency that we are in right now, it hat to be done.”
I had to sit in a corner inside a noisy, stinky warehouse of fish containers. It was not fun.
\doctor: Oh no, sir. There was no issue at all.
\doctor: I hope to be of service as Dr. Pulaski’s Replacement, given the… circumstances.
commander: Did you know Pulaski, Dr. Daphnis?
- WAS A GOOD FRIEND
	jump chapter 1/SHE WAS A GOOD FRIEND
- WAS A GOOD SCIENTIST
	set {fdp} += 1.0
	jump chapter 1/A SHE WAS A GOOD SCIENTIST
label SHE WAS A GOOD FRIEND
\doctor: We met at the Imperial Academy. We got along well. She was not afraid of following her ambitions, and she helped me a lot in my studies.
\doctor: Though we haven’t been much in contact in the last 10 years, I always thought of her as a good friend of mine.
\doctor: It’s a great loss to all those who knew her.
jump chapter 1/main
label A SHE WAS A GOOD SCIENTIST
\doctor: We were colleagues in the Imperial Academy.
\doctor: She had always been an ambitious researcher, ready to jump at any opportunity to work at cutting edge research in the field of foreign lifeforms…
\doctor: The profession has truly lost one of its brightest stars. I can only hope to reach her level of brilliance one day.
label main
commander (suspirando): My condolences then, Doctor. Your friend was a most important crewmember in the Columbus.
commander: She was a very accomplished researcher of foreign life forms, and it’s a great loss to the medical research community.
commander: The work she was doing here was of great interest to the Empire.
\doctor: Thank you sir.
\doctor: ... If I may ask, sir, I was expecting to meet the Captain here today. Is he away?”
commander (brabo): The Captain has been called to an official meeting with the Upper echelons of the Imperial Army.
commander: The details of this meeting, you don’t need to know.”
\doctor: No, sir, of course.
[background arg="res://arts/splash art/backgrounds/corredor.png" fade="0.6"]
I follow Cmd. Flagg down the corridors leading to the lab. He looks slightly amused.
He seems less formal than when we were surrounded by other officers.
commander: Well Doctor, I see you came prepared to make a name for yourself.
\doctor: Pardon me, sir? I’m simply following orders.
commander: Yes… you are… but it’s not every day that a low ranking Medical Officer gets a post at the SS Columbus.
commander: Until now, you’ve been stuck in that backwater of a planet, looking at fish all day, am I right?
\doctor: ...I was leading the research into the marine ecosystems of Tetra 4, yes, sir.
commander: The way you talk, it makes it sound important, doesn’t it?
commander: You’re only here because you’re the closest person with significant expertise to cover for Pulaski in a pinch.
commander: But now it’s your chance to make real work.
commander: You must understand the importance of your role here.
\doctor: Yes, sir.
commander: This is an escort mission for the Empire… a special request from the upper echelons.
commander: You are to make sure a [b]key individual arrives alive[/b] at the destination.
commander: You will update us on its vital signs hourly, and report if anything, and I say ANYTHING, changes.
\doctor: “I understand, sir.”
He slides a pass key on the lab door. I enter after him.
leave commander
audio "res://arts/sfx/vadim_makes_sound-sci-fi-door-unlock-and-hydraulic-open-2-546619.mp3" [volume="-15.0"]
[background arg="res://arts/splash art/backgrounds/laboratorio.png" fade="0.5"]
Just one of the wings of the medical laboratory of the Columbus is much bigger than my small lab at Tetra 4.
There are unfamiliar specimens of several plant and life forms, all cooped up inside large glass containers, immersed in liquid.
Lab assistants and researchers are all over the place, buzzing with nervous energy.
[background arg="res://arts/splash art/backgrounds/laboratorio_zoom.png" fade="0.5"]
We approach the big glass tank. There’s lots of feeding tubes coming out of it.
I know that they carry secretion out of the tank, and take nutrients in.
They all connect to the body floating inside.
I take a good look at it.  They are completely unconscious right now
I have no idea what part of the galaxy they come from. I wonder how they found it…
What is this? Who is this? Why are they important to The Imperial Government? These are questions that I’m a bit shy to ask right now.
Since the war started, they’ve had to cut back on research on foreign civilizations and focus on the war effort.
We can’t afford the level of academic research that we once had… unless it helps the army…
…Something about this makes me uneasy.
commander: Doctor, I want to see you outside for a moment.
audio "res://arts/sfx/vadim_makes_sound-sci-fi-door-unlock-and-hydraulic-open-2-546619.mp3" [volume="-20.0"]
[background arg="res://arts/splash art/backgrounds/corredor.png" fade="0.5"]
join commander (brabo) center
commander: Here, these are your keys now. I don’t need to tell you to keep this under your guard at all times. [br][pause][b]At [pause]all[pause] times[pause], Doctor.[/b]
\doctor: No, sir.
This guy makes me so nervous. Why does he have to be so extra?
commander: I have some last instructions I need to give you, so listen carefully.
commander: This specimen may seem fragile, and you must handle it carefully, but never forget for a moment that it’s [b]dangerous[/b].
\doctor: Dangerous, sir…?
commander: Yes. Treat it like an extremely dangerous object and don’t take your eyes off it for a second.
commander: We don’t know how, or why, but we are sure that that thing is capable of something terrible.
commander: Dr. Pulaski thought that as well… but she didn’t live to figure it out.
\doctor: ...
commander: If you need more encouragement, let me tell you this\:
commander: I will not hesitate to punish an insignificant Doctor Nobody if he fails to protect the safety of the lab, the safety of the ship, and the safety of the Empire.
commander: Do you understand?
\doctor: ...Yes, sir.
commander: Good. [br]I know your record, Doctor.
commander: Your allegiance to the Empire hasn’t always been so shiny, has it? [br]Anthony Daphnis, not always the model citizen?
commander: You look much nicer now, I admit. Easier than being a real man and join the army, I suppose…?
\doctor: ...
commander: Now, now, we don’t have to be hung up on such details. I’m sure you will do your job diligently.
commander: Welcome aboard, Doctor.
leave --All--
[background arg="#000000ff" fade="0.5"]
I can’t breathe.
I go back to the lab, and lock myself in the bathroom, and it’s a while before I can speak to anyone again.
[background arg="res://arts/splash art/backgrounds/laboratorio.png" fade="0.5"]
I’ve been looking at Dr. Pulaski’s documents on the specimen. Species yet unidentified, gender… non applicable?
It’s officially being called L3XA.
I wonder why they didn’t put it’s real name in here. It looks like a sapient species. Can’t it communicate?
L3XA’s vital signs had been fluctuating for a long time, but they’d been improving for the last few weeks.
However, only a couple days ago, all the metabolic processes were increased dramatically, and the report shows lots of unusual activity.
According to my assistants in the lab, Dr. Pulaski and the team spend restless nights trying to stabilize the individual
for the sudden increase in stress after a period of dormancy could have killed it.
She must have had a lot to deal with in her last days, right until the end… I can imagine the stress she was under
with her superiors breathing down her neck, demanding she would save the precious Imperial cargo…
[background arg="#000000ff" fade="0.0"]
commander: “Treat it like an extremely dangerous object and don’t take your eyes off it for a second.”
… Was that what he meant when he said that…? What exactly was Natasha Pulaski dealing with?...
